1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,167
When scheduling assigns berth B7 to vessel
IMO 9321483, billing needs a reservation
signal and the yard board needs a redraw.

2
00:00:09,287 --> 00:00:23,663
Synchronous fan-out from scheduling
couples availability: if billing is down,
does the berth assignment roll back?
Messaging flips the shape. Scheduling
publishes BerthChanged events; consumers
react on their own clocks.

3
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:32,135
Spring Cloud Stream binds your functions
(or suppliers/consumers) to a broker
through a binder — Kafka and RabbitMQ are
the common ones.

4
00:00:32,256 --> 00:00:43,176
You write a Function, Consumer, or
Supplier bean; configuration maps it to
destinations. The business code stays
about events, not about Kafka producer
APIs.

5
00:00:43,295 --> 00:00:59,712
That separation is the point: swap binders
in a test or migrate brokers without
rewriting applyReservation. Look at this
java for a moment. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this example. spring: Walk
the flow.

6
00:00:59,832 --> 00:01:12,528
Scheduling commits the assignment and
sends to berth.changed. Billing’s consumer
group processes the event into an invoice
projection. Yard board’s separate group
redraws independently.

7
00:01:12,647 --> 00:01:25,079
Two groups mean two independent offsets —
billing lag does not freeze the yard
board. If billing is down, messages wait
in the topic; scheduling does not block
the crane operator’s UI on billing’s
health.

8
00:01:25,199 --> 00:01:35,879
Idempotent consumers matter — at-least-
once delivery will redeliver;
applyReservation must tolerate duplicates
by natural key (berthId + window) or an
event id store.

9
00:01:35,999 --> 00:01:45,023
Publishing inside a transaction without an
outbox can still lose messages if the
process dies after commit and before send
— or send before commit and leak phantoms.

10
00:01:45,143 --> 00:01:57,479
Symptom of the first: berth shows ASSIGNED
in scheduling DB, billing never invoices
until a replay tool runs. Symptom of the
second: billing opens an invoice for a
reservation that rolled back.

11
00:01:57,599 --> 00:02:07,583
Treat “after commit + outbox” as the
production upgrade path when loss is
unacceptable — write the event row with
the assignment, relay asynchronously,
delete or mark published.

12
00:02:07,703 --> 00:02:15,239
Failure modes to operate: poison messages
that throw forever need a DLQ binding, or
one bad payload stalls a partition.

13
00:02:15,359 --> 00:02:23,135
Schema changes on BerthChanged need
compatibility discipline — adding optional
fields is safer than renaming imoNumber on
Friday.

14
00:02:23,255 --> 00:02:30,095
Consumer concurrency helps throughput; it
also reorders relative to single-thread
assumptions inside a projection.

15
00:02:30,215 --> 00:02:40,799
Trade-offs: messaging decouples
availability and scales fan-out, at the
cost of eventual consistency and harder
request/response UX. Check-in still wants
a synchronous answer.

16
00:02:40,919 --> 00:02:52,703
Events shine for facts many systems must
learn. Payload size: publish ids and let
billing fetch details inside its context
when payloads would drag vessel master
data across the bus.

17
00:02:52,823 --> 00:03:02,447
A misconception is treating Cloud Stream
as “Kafka with annotations” and ignoring
consumer groups; without groups, competing
consumers do not share work the way you
expect.

18
00:03:02,567 --> 00:03:08,591
Another is dumping huge payloads on the
bus instead of ids and letting consumers
fetch details.

19
00:03:08,711 --> 00:03:20,711
A third is assuming messaging deletes the
need for APIs — check-in still wants
request/response; events shine for fan-out
facts. Cloud patterns alone do not prove
gate release or tariff math.

20
00:03:20,831 --> 00:03:31,151
Phase 10 starts under Spring’s test
annotations, at the engine that discovers
methods, runs them, and reports pass or
fail. That engine is JUnit 5.
